-- title:
--   Left non-degeneracy of the divisorial Weil pairing, assuming divisibility
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and let $F$ be a field that is a $K$-algebra and a $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$-algebra compatibly (so $K \subseteq K(t) \subseteq F$ as a scalar tower) and is finite-dimensional over $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$, with `HasPrincipalDivisors K F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ has a finitely supported divisor $D$ on the places of $F/K$ with $D(v) = v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ for all $v$ and $\deg D = 0$. Fix $n : \mathbb{N}$ with $n \neq 0$. Assume the divisibility hypothesis `hdiv`: for every field $L'$ (in the same universe as $F$) that is likewise a $K$-algebra and a $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$-algebra with the compatible tower and finite over $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$, every nonzero integer divides every element of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(L')$, the group of degree-zero divisors of $L'/K$ modulo principal ones. Let $e$ be any `DivisorialWeilPairingData K F n`, that is: a function $\mathrm{pair} : \mathrm{Pic}^0(F)[n] \times \mathrm{Pic}^0(F)[n] \to K$ which agrees with the pairing attached to each Weil datum $d$ on the pair of classes $(d.\mathrm{classLeft}, d.\mathrm{classRight})$, together with a moving property: every $x \in \mathrm{Pic}^0(F)[n]$ and every finite set $S$ of places admits a degree-zero divisor representing $x$ all of whose support consists of rational places (places whose residue field is generated by the image of $K$) lying outside $S$. The conclusion is that the additive map $e.\mathrm{toHom}$ sending $x$ to the character $y \mapsto e.\mathrm{pair}\,x\,y$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F)[n]$ with values in $K$ is injective.
--
--   This is the non-degeneracy (in the first variable) of the Weil pairing on the $n$-torsion of the Jacobian of a curve, phrased for function fields: the pairing identifies $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F)[n]$ with a subgroup of its own character group. It is used to obtain non-degeneracy for curves over a base field via [`AlgebraicCurve.DivisorialWeilPairingData.toHom_injective_of_isCurveOver`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.DivisorialWeilPairingData.toHom_injective_of_isCurveOver), and in the construction of the (antisymmetric) Weil pairing in [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.exists_weilPairing`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.exists_weilPairing) and [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.exists_antisymmWeilPairing`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.exists_antisymmWeilPairing).
